  "name": "THYROGLOBULIN",
  "code": "1792",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>After thyroidectomy presence of elevated Tg gives a indication of recurrence of metastatic disease. Thyroglobulin is used as a tumor marker for thyroid cancer to evaluate the effectiveness of treatment and its recurrence. It can also occasionally help in determining the cause of hyperthyroidism and to monitor the effectiveness of treatment. It is not specific for thyroid cancer and can be elevated in both benign and malignant thyroid diseases.<br><strong>Increased values:</strong> Residual thyroid tissue after thyroidectomy, recurrence of thyroid cancer or metastasis. It can also be elevated in Graves&rsquo;s disease, hyperthyroidism, goiter, and thyroiditis.<br><strong>Decreased values :</strong> Post thyroidectomy or Post treatment for Grave&rsquo;s disease.</p>\n<p><br><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Not all thyroid cancer will show elevated thyroglobulin levels.</li>\n<li>Serial determination of thyroglobulin levels is more important than a single value.</li>\n<li>Increased concentration of thyroglobulin is not diagnosis of cancer.</li>\n<li>Values may vary depending on the methodology used for testing.</li>\n<li>Thyroglobulin Antibodies in the patient&rsquo;s sample can interfere with the result. In such cases thyroglobulin test is rendered ineffective.</li>\n</ul>",
